# ============================================================

A comparative-effectiveness project must pass:

### 1. Clinical equipoise
Would a reasonable oncologist genuinely consider either option for similar patients?

### 2. Correct exposure
Manual audit ≥95%.

### 3. Correct treatment line
Manual audit ≥95%.

### 4. Adequate N
Prefer ≥40/arm.
30–39 potentially feasible.
20–29 exploratory.
<20 reject.

### 5. Positivity
Common support preferably ≥80%.
70–79% marginal.
<70% major concern.

### 6. Balance
Clinically important weighted |SMD| <0.10.

### 7. Major confounder measurement
No obvious treatment-deterministic variable completely missing.

### 8. Date robustness
Conclusion stable to ±6-month shifting sensitivity.

### 9. Outcome information
Adequate event count/follow-up.

### 10. Contemporary relevance
Do not recommend obsolete treatment comparisons simply because N is large.

Failure of a critical gate should prevent "recommended primary causal project" status.

